        "original_id": "a6d129d7-e0d0-5572-9d2c-e5fa4582b27d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Herbert Lionel Matthews, Fidel Castro, Fulgencio Batista\n- Organizations: The New York Times, 26th of July Movement\n- Places: Sierra Maestra, Cuba\n- Events: Cuban Revolution\n- Roles: reporter, editorialist, president, dictator\n\nFacts:\n- Herbert Lionel Matthews was born on January 10, 1900, and died on July 30, 1977.\n- Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar, born Rubén Zaldívar, was born on January 16, 1901, and died on August 6, 1973.\n- Fulgencio Batista was the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944.\n- Herbert Lionel Matthews was a reporter and editorialist for The New York Times.\n- Fulgencio Batista was a U.S.-backed dictator of Cuba from 1952 to 1959.\n- Herbert Lionel Matthews became notorious after revealing that Fidel Castro was alive and living in the Sierra Maestra mountains.\n- Fulgencio Batista had publicly claimed that Fidel Castro was killed during the 26th of July Movement's landing.\n- Fulgencio Batista was overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "26ffbd9c-78d4-5154-bdb2-63e437e6dd60",
        "text": "Page: Herbert Matthews. Sentence: Herbert Lionel Matthews (January 10, 1900 – July 30, 1977) was a reporter and editorialist for \"The New York Times\" who grew to notoriety after revealing that Fidel Castro was still alive and living in the Sierra Maestra mountains, though Fulgencio Batista had claimed publicly that he was killed during the 26th of July Movement's landing.\nPage: Fulgencio Batista. Sentence: Fulgencio Batista y Zaldívar (] ; born Rubén Zaldívar; January 16, 1901 – August 6, 1973) was the elected President of Cuba from 1940 to 1944, and U.S.-backed dictator from 1952 to 1959, before being overthrown during the Cuban Revolution.",
